  <p><strong>How do I avoid mismatched panels and track fabric shade problems in bulk activewear?</strong></p>

  <h2>Catch a mismatch before pieces are cut</h2>
  <p>Read the roll labels and compare swatches under agreed lighting. A colour name or identical dye lot label may be insufficient if roll-to-roll appearance varies. Ask the supplier which ends or sections are sampled and agree what to do if darker and lighter rolls arrive together.</p>

  <h2>Control what is mixed in one garment</h2>
  <p>For a two-tone garment, record the intended contrast. For a single-colour garment, keep panels and matching components within the approved shade group when possible. Evaluate tops and bottoms together if they are sold as sets, especially when made from different fabric constructions.</p>

  <h2>Keep enough information to investigate later</h2>
  <p>Record the order, shade group, roll IDs, cut batch and affected carton or SKU where practical. If a mismatch appears during packing or receiving, this record helps distinguish a limited run from a wider batch issue. Agree who owns the record and what evidence to share before release.</p>

  <p>Industry reference: <a href="https://linkedsourcing.com/manufacturing/">Linked Sourcing separates fabric-roll controls, bulk shade approval and final inspection in its manufacturing guide.</a></p>
